# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in real values.

# Supabase project: Settings -> API
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Supabase project: Settings -> API Keys -> Secret keys.
# Server-only - bypasses Row Level Security. Never expose to the browser
# (never prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_) and never commit the real value.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Razorpay: Dashboard -> Settings -> API Keys.
# Use Test Mode keys (rzp_test_...) until the full payment flow has been
# verified end to end - only switch to Live Mode keys after that.
# The Key ID is not secret (it's sent to the browser to open the checkout
# widget); the Key Secret must stay server-only.
NEXT_PUBLIC_RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=

# Razorpay webhook signing secret: Dashboard -> Settings -> Webhooks.
# Create a webhook pointing at https://<your-domain>/api/razorpay/webhook
# subscribed to payment.captured (payment.authorized and payment.failed are
# recorded too), and paste the secret you set there here. This is a
# different secret from RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET above.
#
# Without it /api/razorpay/webhook answers 503 and payment confirmation
# falls back to the browser callback alone -- which means a patient who
# pays and closes the tab before returning to the site leaves a paid order
# against an unpaid booking. Set it in every environment that takes real
# payments.
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Google Calendar / Meet integration. OAuth2 Client credentials from Google
# Cloud Console (Web application type), a dedicated Calendar's ID, and a
# refresh token obtained by running `node scripts/get-google-refresh-token.mjs`
# once (see that script's header comment for the one-time setup steps).
# The authorizing Google account acts as the meeting organizer.
#
# Enable BOTH the Google Calendar API and the Google Meet API on the Cloud
# project. The refresh token carries two scopes: calendar.events creates the
# session's event and Meet link, and meetings.space.settings is what opens
# each meeting so neither the patient nor the therapist is held in a waiting
# room. A token minted before that second scope keeps creating events and
# returns 403 on the access change -- sessions then show under
# Settings -> System Health -> Waiting Room until the script is re-run.
GOOGLE_CALENDAR_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CALENDAR_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_CALENDAR_REFRESH_TOKEN=
GOOGLE_CALENDAR_ID=

# Optional - a Supabase Personal Access Token (supabase.com -> Account ->
# Access Tokens), NOT the service role key or DB password. Only needed to
# run `node scripts/run-schema.mjs`, which applies supabase/schema.sql over
# the Supabase Management API instead of pasting it into the SQL Editor.
SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# Pre-launch testing only. When set to exactly "true", the debug bar's
# "Reset data" button works: it empties every table and deletes every
# non-admin account, keeping only admin logins. Unset (the default), the
# route answers 404 and the button reports that it is switched off.
#
# NEVER set this on a deployment holding real patient data. It is separate
# from NEXT_PUBLIC_SHOW_DEBUG_NAV on purpose -- the debug bar is on in every
# environment while the app is pre-launch, so reusing that flag would arm a
# data wipe for anyone who can reach the page.
ALLOW_DEBUG_DATA_RESET=

# Optional. The debug bar's kill switch. The bar is on everywhere by default
# (src/lib/debugNavVisible.ts) because the app is pre-launch; set this to
# exactly "false" to hide it without touching code. At real launch, delete
# the bar rather than switching it off -- this flag is public, so the routes
# it names are readable by anyone.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SHOW_DEBUG_NAV=

# Optional. The ceiling on how many HTTP requests this server may have in
# flight to Supabase at once, per instance (default 96), and the deadline on
# each one in milliseconds (default 20000).
#
# These exist because Node's fetch opens as many sockets as it is asked to.
# A load test at 40 concurrent admin dashboard renders -- ~82 queries each,
# so ~3,300 requests in flight against one origin -- pushed TLS handshakes
# past undici's 10-second connect timeout, and the dashboard's own isolated
# guards then rendered the missing data as zeroes: fifteen renders answered
# HTTP 200 having silently lost the appointments table. The database itself
# answered 400 concurrent requests in 4.6s with no errors in the same run,
# so the origin was never the limit.
#
# The default was measured. At 40 concurrent renders: 48 gave a p50 of 130s,
# 96 gave 15.3s, 192 gave 16.4s. Raise it only if the database can take more
# connections than it can today; lowering it below ~64 makes things worse,
# because a page render is a chain of query batches and every sequential
# step pays the queue's full depth again.
SUPABASE_MAX_IN_FLIGHT=
SUPABASE_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=

# Optional. How many Node processes `npm run start:cluster` runs on one port.
# Defaults to the number of cores, capped at 4.
#
# One `next start` renders React on one thread, and that is the app's ceiling
# once the database stops being one: with 200 concurrent visitors browsing
# the public pages, one admin dashboard render went from 4.0s to 15.9s while
# Supabase held a flat ~290ms a query throughout. Four workers took the
# public site from 395 to 580 requests a second and that dashboard render
# back to 11.3s.
#
# It is capped rather than "one per core" because the lazy sweeps
# (retryDueMeetSyncs, retryDueMeetAccess, runRiskSweep) keep their minimum
# intervals in module state, so N workers means up to N sweeps per window.
# That is safe -- each claims its rows and each row carries its own attempt
# cap -- but it spends an appointment's automatic retries faster.
#
# SUPABASE_MAX_IN_FLIGHT is divided across the workers unless you set it
# yourself; see scripts/start-cluster.mjs for the measurements behind that.
WEB_CONCURRENCY=

# Optional. How many proxies sit in front of this app. Defaults to 0, meaning
# "the last hop is the one I trust".
#
# The public rate limiter prefers `x-real-ip`. Where that header is absent it
# falls back to `x-forwarded-for` and reads the list from the RIGHT, because a
# proxy *appends* the address it saw: the leftmost entry is whatever the
# original caller claimed, and the rightmost is the only one a trusted hop
# actually observed. Reading from the left -- which this used to do -- meant
# every request was keyed on a value the caller chose, so each one got a fresh
# allowance and every public cap was off while appearing to work.
#
# How far in from the right to read is the one thing only the operator knows.
# Leave it unset behind a single proxy. Set it to 1 if another proxy sits in
# front of that one, and so on.
#
# Wrong upward puts several visitors in one bucket, which Settings -> System
# Health -> "Public doors" reports. Wrong downward is what this removed, and
# no value can reintroduce it: the number is clamped so it cannot walk past
# the start of the list into caller-supplied territory.
RATE_LIMIT_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=

# Optional, server-only. Signs the proxy's 60-second cache of the three
# profile fields the dashboard guards read (role, approved, active).
#
# Any long random string -- `openssl rand -base64 32` is fine. NEVER prefix
# this with NEXT_PUBLIC_, and never commit a real value: the whole guarantee
# is that a browser cannot mint a cookie claiming to be an admin, and a
# published secret is not a secret.
#
# Leave it unset and the cache is simply off: src/lib/supabase/proxy.ts falls
# back to reading `profiles` on every dashboard request, exactly as it did
# before. Missing makes the app slower, never wrong and never open.
#
# Setting it accepts one trade-off, stated in full in
# src/lib/proxyProfileCache.ts: for up to 60 seconds, a session that is
# ALREADY OPEN keeps the role and flags it had when the cookie was written,
# so suspending or demoting an account takes up to a minute to lock out a tab
# that is already on a dashboard. New sign-ins are unaffected.
PROXY_PROFILE_CACHE_SECRET=

# Optional. How many seconds the cookie above is trusted. Defaults to 60.
#
# This is a security setting dressed as a performance one. It is how long a
# suspension, demotion or revoked approval can go unnoticed by a dashboard
# tab that is ALREADY OPEN -- a fresh sign-in is always checked live. Lower
# it where that lag matters more than the speed; 15 keeps most of the
# benefit.
#
# Clamped to 5-300 and ignored unless it parses to a positive number, so a
# mistyped 86400 holds a revoked admin open for five minutes rather than a
# day, and a blank value reads as "unset" rather than as 0. Raising the
# ceiling means changing MAX_PROFILE_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS in
# src/lib/proxyProfileCache.ts -- a reviewed commit, deliberately, rather
# than a text box.
#
# Does nothing without PROXY_PROFILE_CACHE_SECRET: no secret, no cache, no
# window to size.
PROXY_PROFILE_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=

# Shared secret for /api/cron/maintenance, the scheduled housekeeping
# (programme expiry, Meet retries, waiting rooms, the risk scan). Server-only:
# never prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_. Set the same value as the CRON_SECRET
# repository secret used by .github/workflows/maintenance.yml, together with
# MAINTENANCE_URL (the deployed origin). Unset, the route answers 503 and the
# sweep still runs whenever an admin opens the dashboard.
CRON_SECRET=
